	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44397</id><title>Were Half of Venezuela’s Oil Revenues Really Stolen? — Part I</title><updated>2026-10-21T17:08:51-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44397" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Atardecer_-_Lagunillas_-_Costa_Oriental_del_Lago_de_Maracaibo__.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Atardecer_-_Lagunillas_-_Costa_Oriental_del_Lago_de_Maracaibo__.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Atardecer_-_Lagunillas_-_Costa_Oriental_del_Lago_de_Maracaibo__-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Atardecer_-_Lagunillas_-_Costa_Oriental_del_Lago_de_Maracaibo__.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
DENVER, Colorado, USA, October 21 (IPS)  - If you’ve been around Venezuelans for long enough, you know that we tend to speak in superlatives. We have the best baseball players, the most beautiful beaches, the tastiest arepas, and the wickedest dictators. But even being used to the scale of our superlatives didn’t quite prepare me for what I read in the May 5 edition of The New York Times, in a &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/05/world/trump-venezuela-oil-deals.html&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;story&lt;/a&gt; called “In Venezuela, Trump Vowed to Show Accountability. Oil Deals Linger.”
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44397&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Were Half of Venezuela’s Oil Revenues Really Stolen? — Part I”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44394</id><title>The Sámi People and Reindeer Herding – a Struggle for Sápmi’s Whole Nature</title><updated>2026-10-21T10:21:30-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44394" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Sapmi-2026_DSC7109_Johan-Mikaelsson.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Sapmi-2026_DSC7109_Johan-Mikaelsson.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Sapmi-2026_DSC7109_Johan-Mikaelsson-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Sapmi-2026_DSC7109_Johan-Mikaelsson.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
SÁPMI, Norway, October 21 (IPS)  - The Sámi, Europe’s last recognised Indigenous community, have faced threats to their existence throughout history. Norway last year has apologised for its forced assimilation policy following the findings of a Truth and Reconciliation Commission report, and in September 2026 Sweden has recently been told to do the same.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44394&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Sámi People and Reindeer Herding – a Struggle for Sápmi’s Whole Nature”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44393</id><title>As Winter Approaches, Afghanistan Faces Record Hunger and Mounting Human Rights Abuses</title><updated>2026-10-21T09:02:44-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44393" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Communities-face-multi_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Communities-face-multi_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Communities-face-multi_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/Communities-face-multi_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, October 21 (IPS)  - In recent months, Afghanistan’s humanitarian crisis has severely deteriorated, with the UN reporting record-high levels of acute malnutrition and hunger. Compounded by rampant human rights violations, limited access to basic services, and persistent barriers to humanitarian aid, the UN warns that conditions could considerably worsen in the coming months.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44393&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “As Winter Approaches, Afghanistan Faces Record Hunger and Mounting Human Rights Abuses”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44404</id><title>Fate unknown of many returned to DPR Korea from China: UN independent experts</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44404" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-12-2023-Unsplash-DPRK.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-12-2023-Unsplash-DPRK.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-12-2023-Unsplash-DPRK.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-12-2023-Unsplash-DPRK.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Three years on from the reported resumption of large-scale forcible returns of nationals of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea (DPRK) from China, serious concerns persist about their fate, according to a group of Human Rights Council-appointed independent experts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44404&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Fate unknown of many returned to DPR Korea from China: UN independent experts”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44403</id><title>‘Ominous start’ to West Bank olive harvest amid settler attacks</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44403" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/21-10-2025-PARC-olive-harvest-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/21-10-2025-PARC-olive-harvest-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/21-10-2025-PARC-olive-harvest-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/21-10-2025-PARC-olive-harvest-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The use of live ammunition, arson and the destruction of centuries-old olive trees have marked the opening weeks of this year’s harvest in the occupied West Bank.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44403&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Ominous start’ to West Bank olive harvest amid settler attacks”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44402</id><title>World News in Brief: Aid for Ethiopia, Abyei drone strikes condemned, extreme heat and newborn health</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44402" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-04-2024-UNICEF-Ethiopia-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-04-2024-UNICEF-Ethiopia-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-04-2024-UNICEF-Ethiopia-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/19-04-2024-UNICEF-Ethiopia-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is scaling up emergency health response operations across Afar, Amhara and Tigray in Ethiopia to address growing humanitarian and health needs resulting from ongoing hostilities.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44402&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “World News in Brief: Aid for Ethiopia, Abyei drone strikes condemned, extreme heat and newborn health”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44401</id><title>Migrants paid up to $500 million in smuggling fees on European sea routes</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44401" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/23-10-2015-SOS-mediterranean-12.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/23-10-2015-SOS-mediterranean-12.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/23-10-2015-SOS-mediterranean-12.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/23-10-2015-SOS-mediterranean-12.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The price tag for smuggling on European sea routes reached up to half a billion dollars last year, the UN Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) and the UN migration office, IOM, reported on Wednesday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44401&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Migrants paid up to $500 million in smuggling fees on European sea routes”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44400</id><title>Summit in Samarkand, a boon for child wellbeing and health</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44400" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21-10-2026_Samarkand%20Forum%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21-10-2026_Samarkand%20Forum%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21-10-2026_Samarkand%20Forum%202.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21-10-2026_Samarkand%20Forum%202.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Representatives from across the international community are meeting in Samarkand, Uzbekistan, to ensure that children everywhere can thrive in a world marred by war, climate chaos and other fundamental challenges.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44400&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Summit in Samarkand, a boon for child wellbeing and health”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44399</id><title>UN agencies appeal for help to protect weakest from unprecedented El Niño</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44399" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/05-10-2021-UNICEF-UN0403971-Mozambique.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/05-10-2021-UNICEF-UN0403971-Mozambique.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/05-10-2021-UNICEF-UN0403971-Mozambique.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/05-10-2021-UNICEF-UN0403971-Mozambique.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Two UN agencies issued a powerful appeal on Wednesday for immediate assistance to help communities already being impacted by the strongest El Niño weather pattern on record.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44399&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “UN agencies appeal for help to protect weakest from unprecedented El Niño”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44398</id><title>Sudan conflict: Peace efforts resume to end ‘drone war’ fuelling humanitarian crisis</title><updated>2026-10-21T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44398" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21_10_26_UNOG_Sudan_meeting_1.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21_10_26_UNOG_Sudan_meeting_1.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21_10_26_UNOG_Sudan_meeting_1.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/21_10_26_UNOG_Sudan_meeting_1.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Talks to halt the fighting in Sudan and alleviate the humanitarian crisis there continued in Geneva on Wednesday, where nearly two dozen countries convened to discuss a pathway to a ceasefire and transition to peaceful governance that represents all Sudanese people. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/21/44398&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Sudan conflict: Peace efforts resume to end ‘drone war’ fuelling humanitarian crisis”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0015s ch:1 -->